  "report_markdown": "DRAFT\n\n**Complaint/History**\n- Diarrhea for the last three days, described as loose and watery stools.\n- Bowel frequency: 6–7 times per day.\n- Lower abdominal pain, localized to the left side, described as cramp-like, intermittent.\n- Vomiting at symptom onset, now resolved; vomit was food-colored, no blood.\n- Feeling weak and shaky; no measured fever, but felt hot at symptom onset (~3–4 days ago).\n- Loss of appetite; able to tolerate fluids and light foods (soups, smoothies).\n- Symptoms began after eating takeaway food from a Chinese restaurant four days ago.\n- No other household members have diarrhea; one child had vomiting but not diarrhea.\n- Symptoms affecting daily activities, requiring proximity to toilet; patient has continued working as an accountant.\n\n**Relevant Background**\n- Asthma, well-controlled with inhaler.\n- No other known gastrointestinal conditions.\n- No other medications besides inhaler.\n- Non-smoker, does not consume alcohol.\n\n**Findings**\n- No blood in stool or vomit.\n- Pain is localized to left lower abdomen, crampy, intermittent; does not radiate to back.\n- No current fever, sweating, or other systemic symptoms.\n- Able to maintain hydration with fluids.\n\n**Clinician's Assessment**\n- Likely acute gastroenteritis, possibly foodborne (viral or bacterial).\n- No signs of severe infection or dehydration requiring immediate intervention.\n\n**Plan/Follow-up**\n- Conservative management: oral rehydration (e.g., Dioralyte).\n- Paracetamol 2 tablets up to four times daily for fever and weakness in first few days.\n- Advised to take 2–3 days off work to rest.\n- Return for follow-up if symptoms persist beyond 3–4 days.\n- Consider stool sample testing if symptoms continue.",
